spawn-fcgi 1.6.6 binds a Unix socket under a name one byte short on the
BSD-derived systems: macOS, FreeBSD, OpenBSD, NetBSD.

Its source defines _XOPEN_SOURCE 600, a request for the strict POSIX
namespace. On those systems that hides the SUN_LEN macro of <sys/un.h>, and
bind_socket falls back to strlen(sun_path) + sizeof(sun_family). Their
struct sockaddr_un starts with a one-byte sun_len before a one-byte
sun_family, so that sum is one byte short of where the path ends. The
kernel takes the path's length from the length passed to bind, and binds
the name without its last character. The chmod of the name asked for then
fails with ENOENT. Linux is not affected: glibc honours _GNU_SOURCE, which
the file defines first and which overrides _XOPEN_SOURCE, so SUN_LEN stays
visible there and the fallback is never compiled.

offsetof(struct sockaddr_un, sun_path) + strlen(sun_path) is what SUN_LEN
computes, on every platform.

--- a/src/spawn-fcgi.c
+++ b/src/spawn-fcgi.c
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include <sys/time.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 
+#include <stddef.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <string.h>
 #include <errno.h>
@@ -94,12 +95,7 @@
 		if (strlen(unixsocket) > sizeof(fcgi_addr_un.sun_path) - 1) return -1;
 		strcpy(fcgi_addr_un.sun_path, unixsocket);
 
-#ifdef SUN_LEN
-		servlen = SUN_LEN(&fcgi_addr_un);
-#else
-		/* stevens says: */
-		servlen = strlen(fcgi_addr_un.sun_path) + sizeof(fcgi_addr_un.sun_family);
-#endif
+		servlen = offsetof(struct sockaddr_un, sun_path) + strlen(fcgi_addr_un.sun_path);
 		socket_type = AF_UNIX;
 		fcgi_addr = (struct sockaddr *) &fcgi_addr_un;
 
